---
import FlightScene021 from "../../../components/studies/FlightScene021.svelte";
import FlightScene022 from "../../../components/studies/FlightScene022.svelte";
import FlightScene023 from "../../../components/studies/FlightScene023.svelte";
import FlightScene024 from "../../../components/studies/FlightScene024.svelte";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "flight-scene-instrument-2026-11-10",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section aria-labelledby="problem">
    <h2 id="problem">01 / Every study builds its own window into the game</h2>
    <p>
      A study about an engine plume, a cannon ray or a gravity fold has to show
      the thing in flight. Each one so far has built that view itself. The
      <a href="/lab/studies/ion-engines-2026-10-31">ion engine</a>
      streams, the
      <a href="/lab/studies/lhi-wasp#flight-demo">Wasp flight demo</a>, the
      <a href="/lab/studies/base-movement-ion">stabilizer flight</a>, the
      <a href="/lab/studies/gravity-coil-fx-2026-11-03">gravity fold flyby</a>
      and the
      <a href="/lab/studies/light-ion-cannon-2026-11-08">Light Ion Cannon</a>
      ray each chose their own background, effects and playback controls. Two
      shared a flight script and one borrowed the Motion instrument’s burns; the
      rest wrote their own. One drew the hull by hand, several flew a private
      approximation of the physics, and none composed every layer the game
      draws. The effect is judged against a different picture every time, and
      none of those pictures is quite the game.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Flight scene instrument is the Motion instrument’s sibling. It keeps
      the same burns and the same time slider, and the same full-window band
      that snaps into the reading flow. Where the Motion instrument draws
      graphs, this one gives the screen to a single large view of the game’s own
      layers: stars, ship, engine effects, the light surface and weapon fire,
      drawn by the production renderers from the production simulation. An agent
      configures the ship and the scene; the designer picks a burn, moves
      through time and looks. The Light Ion Cannon study planted the seed: it
      put a small game view in the Motion instrument’s path pane and repainted
      its light from the start of the burn whenever the cursor moved back. The
      scene has the game’s gravity and power limits, so a burn flies a different
      path here than on the Motion instrument’s graphs.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tension is between a picture that can be inspected and a picture that
      is true. The game’s light is not a function of the moment: the effects
      surface is fading pixel history, sparks and the camera carry state, and
      weapons fire by themselves only when a target is within their reach and
      the ship has power. A time slider asks for any instant on demand; the game
      can only arrive at an instant by flying there. How the instrument
      reconciles the two decides what a study can trust it to show.
    </p>
    <h3>Held fixed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The Motion instrument’s burn programs B1, B2 and B3, their 14 seconds
        and the time cursor that selects an instant.
      </li>
      <li>
        The full-window, snapping study embed and the pane that holds the values
        under investigation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Production simulation and renderers, drawn in the game’s order. The
        instrument adds no ship art, effect or physics of its own, and later
        production changes show up in it. The order itself is written only
        inside the game’s flight screen today, so the instrument copies it until
        it is shared.
      </li>
      <li>
        The agent supplies the configuration; the designer adjusts only what the
        study exposes.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Left open</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The screen: how large the scene is, and where the burn row, time and
        playback sit around it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Time: what a jump to an instant shows of light that depends on the
        preceding moments, and whether the scene also plays.
      </li>
      <li>
        The camera: the game’s framing and zoom jumps, a fixed view, or a
        choice.
      </li>
      <li>
        The scene around the ship: what a configuration can change in the game’s
        arena (where the ship starts, which bodies are present, targets that
        make weapons fire) and which layers the designer can switch off to
        isolate one.
      </li>
      <li>Whether one ship is enough or a comparison belongs on the screen.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Outside this cycle</h3>
    <p>
      Moving earlier studies’ benches onto the instrument, a permanent Lab room
      for it, flying it by hand with the keyboard, and new burns that include
      weapon inputs are adjacent questions. The game has no fire button, so a
      burn stays an engine program.
    </p>
    <aside class="stamp" aria-labelledby="stamp">
      <h3 id="stamp">Question check · 10 November 2026</h3>
      <p>
        An independent reviewer with no part in the framing tested this section
        as first written. Grounded failed on how the section described what
        exists, not on whether it exists; the text above was corrected
        afterwards and has not been tested again.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Metric</th>
            <th scope="col">Result</th>
            <th scope="col">Reason</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">One subject</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              One object, the instrument. The five open items are facets of its
              screen, and four adjacent questions are named.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Grounded</th>
            <td>Fail</td>
            <td>
              Every fixed item exists and every open item can be shown. The
              section overstated how far the earlier views differed, left out
              the Light Ion Cannon study’s scene in the Motion instrument,
              called the draw order shared when it is written in one place, and
              listed the time cursor as fixed while leaving scrubbing open.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Consequence</th>
            <td>Pass</td>
            <td>
              Studies judge flight on one shared screen instead of building
              their own. The change is to the Lab, not the game, which the
              test’s usual wording does not cover.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </aside>
  </section>
  <details id="time">
    <summary>
      02.1 / Can the game’s light be reached by a jump? — yes, by flying there
    </summary>
    <section>
      <p>
        <strong>Settled, 10 November 2026.</strong>
        A jump flies the burn again from its start and paints the whole flight’s
        light, so the frame it lands on is the frame playing would have shown.
        The scene both plays and jumps. The setting in this example’s menu stays
        as the evidence; the instrument itself has no such setting.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first example takes the time question, because every other choice
        rests on it. The hypothesis: a jump can show the frame the game would
        have shown, if the instrument flies the burn again from its start to
        reach it. Light on the effects surface halves every tenth of a second,
        so painting only the last second of that flight should be enough.
      </p>
      <p>
        Press Play, or drag Time to jump. The menu in the top-right corner sets
        what a jump paints: only the instant it lands on, the last second before
        it, or the whole flight. Compare the three at a moment when the engine
        or the cannon is lit, such as 1.67 s on B1.
      </p>
      <div class="bleed"><FlightScene021 client:visible /></div>
      <p>
        The scene is the game’s arena under Derelicts rules, with the encounter
        held off: sun, planets, boundary, starfield and the game camera, drawn
        in the flight screen’s order. The starter Wasp carries a Light Ion
        Cannon on its first hull mount and starts at rest where a run starts.
        Two wrecks are placed ahead of it so the cannon fires. Nothing is
        random. The screen borrows the Motion instrument’s size, burn row and
        slider; its layout is provisional.
      </p>
      <h3>Measured</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          At nine instants on B1, a jump painting the last second differs from
          one painting the whole flight in at most 7 of 2,073,600 pixels.
        </li>
        <li>
          Painting only the landing instant differs in 800 to 16,000 pixels
          whenever an engine or the cannon is lit: the plume and the ray’s
          afterglow are missing.
        </li>
        <li>
          A jump to the end of the burn takes about 0.2 s. Almost all of it is
          flying the simulation; painting the whole flight’s light adds about a
          seventh.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These figures come from one machine on 10 November 2026. Painting the
        whole flight runs the same code as playing, tick for tick, but the two
        were not compared pixel by pixel. The figures say a jump can be
        faithful; they do not say whether a still instant is a useful way to
        judge an effect that the player only ever sees moving.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two things show up that belong to later rounds. The game camera zooms
        out as the ship climbs away from the sun, so the ship is small for most
        of the burn. And the first wreck is destroyed within two seconds, after
        which the cannon has nothing to fire at.
      </p>
    </section>
  </details>
  <details id="camera">
    <summary>
      02.2 / What should the scene be looked at through? — the game camera and
      two follow views
    </summary>
    <section>
      <p>
        <strong>Settled, 10 November 2026.</strong>
        The instrument has three views: Game, Follow and Follow 4×. An
        enlargement of the game’s own pixels was compared and not kept.
      </p>
      <p>
        The game camera frames the ship and the sun together and jumps between
        five zoom levels. That framing is part of what the player sees, but it
        leaves the ship about twenty pixels long for most of a burn, too small
        to judge a plume or a ray by. The two follow views move the camera in
        and hold the ship at the centre.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Game.</strong>
          The game camera, as in 02.1: true framing, true zoom jumps, small
          ship.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Follow.</strong>
          The ship held at the centre at the game’s closest zoom level, 1.6. A
          scale the game does reach, without the framing or the jumps.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Follow 4×.</strong>
          The ship at the centre at a zoom the game never reaches. Ship art and
          light are drawn at that scale, so edges and afterglow are finer than
          in play. What looks right here still has to be checked in Game.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bleed"><FlightScene022 client:visible /></div>
      <p>
        The light surface is tied to the camera, so switching the view flies the
        burn again. Scene, ship, wrecks and time model are those of 02.1. The
        switch sits on the scene for this comparison; where it belongs is a
        layout question, as is whether the two follow zooms stay fixed at 1.6
        and 4.
      </p>
    </section>
  </details>
  <details id="scene">
    <summary>
      02.3 / What does a study set around the ship? — its start, its targets and
      the layers shown
    </summary>
    <section>
      <p>
        <strong>Settled, 10 November 2026.</strong>
        A configuration sets where the ship starts and what it has to fire at,
        including a wreck held ahead of it for the whole burn. The designer can
        hide each of the three layers.
      </p>
      <p>
        So far the ship starts where a run starts and two wrecks sit ahead of
        it. That scene has two limits, both seen in 02.1: the first wreck is
        gone within two seconds, after which the cannon is silent, and every
        burn is judged against the same patch of the arena. This example puts
        three things a configuration could set into the menu in the top-right
        corner, to see which of them the instrument needs.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Where the ship starts.</strong>
          At rest, at the run’s start, close to the sun, or far out. The arena,
          its sun and its planets stay the game’s own; only the ship’s first
          position and heading move.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Targets.</strong>
          None; two wrecks placed ahead of the start, as before; or one wreck
          held 140 units ahead of the ship for the whole burn and replaced each
          time it breaks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Layers.</strong>
          World, FX and LFX are the game’s three stacked layers. Each can be
          hidden to look at the others alone.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bleed"><FlightScene023 client:visible /></div>
      <p>
        The held wreck is where the instrument steps in: it moves the wreck with
        the ship every tick and replaces it when it breaks. The cannon, its
        damage, the break and the shards are the game’s. The game keeps shards
        for good, and a wreck replaced for a whole burn buried the scene in
        them, so the held wreck’s shards are removed two seconds after release.
        Placed wrecks keep the game’s behaviour. Hiding a layer hides its
        canvas; the layer is still drawn, so the picture underneath is
        unchanged.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where the ship starts decides whether a burn can be flown at all. Flown
        to the end on 10 November 2026: from the run’s start B3 ends in the sun
        at about 10 s; close to the sun B2 ends there at about 7 s and B3 at
        about 14 s; far out all three burns complete. The scene says when the
        ship is lost. These were read once from the running page and not
        repeated.
      </p>
    </section>
  </details>
  <section aria-labelledby="layout">
    <h2 id="layout">
      02.4 / How is the screen arranged? — the scene is the screen
    </h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Settled, 10 November 2026.</strong>
      The scene fills the instrument’s whole screen. Burn and time float over
      its lower edge, the view switch sits in its top-left corner and the values
      stay in the menu in the top-right.
    </p>
    <p>
      The examples before this one borrowed the Motion instrument’s arrangement:
      a 16:9 scene above a row of controls. Two alternatives were compared with
      the one kept here and not retained: that arrangement, and a 4:3 scene
      beside a rail that always showed the view and the values. The rail kept
      everything in sight and nothing over the picture, at the cost of about two
      fifths of its area.
    </p>
    <div class="bleed"><FlightScene024 client:visible /></div>
    <p>
      The scene is drawn at 16:10. The game draws at any width for its fixed
      height, so this is a picture the game can show. The menu still opens over
      the right half of the scene while a value is being changed.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="ships">
    <h2 id="ships">02.5 / One ship or a comparison? — one ship</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Settled, 10 November 2026.</strong>
      The instrument shows one ship. A study that compares two engines or two
      loadouts embeds two instruments one after the other. Splitting the screen
      would halve the picture that 02.4 chose to make as large as possible. No
      example was built for this; it follows from 02.4.
    </p>
    <h3>What part 02 settled</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Time.</strong>
        The scene plays and jumps. A jump flies the burn again from its start
        and paints the whole flight’s light.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Camera.</strong>
        Game, Follow at 1.6 and Follow 4×; switching flies the burn again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Scene.</strong>
        A configuration sets the ship’s start and its targets: none, wrecks
        placed in the arena, or one wreck held 140 units ahead whose shards are
        removed after two seconds. The designer can hide World, FX and LFX.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Screen.</strong>
        The scene fills the 1440 × 900 screen at 16:10. Burn and time float over
        its lower edge, the view switch is top-left, the values menu top-right.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Ships.</strong> One per screen.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The reference is the 02.4 example: the starter Wasp with a Light Ion
      Cannon on its first hull mount, Derelicts rules with the encounter held
      off, burns B1 to B3 at 60 ticks a second, no randomness. The two follow
      zooms, the 140 units and the two seconds are working values, not tuned
      ones.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="readiness">
    <h2 id="readiness">03 / The parts exist; the instrument is not yet one</h2>
    <p>
      The intended delivery is a shared instrument for studies: a study embeds
      it with one configuration, the way it embeds the Motion instrument, and a
      page in the Lab shows it in its default setting as the reference, in the
      design-system sense, for anyone who uses it. Everything the examples do
      runs on production code today. What is missing is the step from four study
      examples to one shared component with a contract.
    </p>
    <h3>Reusable as it stands</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The production simulation, and the game’s frame: since the layer split
        the flight screen and this study draw through the same ordered passes
        onto the same three stacked layers, with the game’s blending and bloom.
        The draw order is no longer copied by hand, which section 01 had to
        allow for.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Motion instrument’s burn programs, burn row, time slider, switches,
        values menu and the full-window snapping band.
      </li>
      <li>
        The study’s scene engine: replay by flying, the three views, the start
        position, placed and held targets, and a render width that follows the
        screen’s shape.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Needs adapting</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The engine and the 02.4 screen live among the study’s components. They
        move beside the Motion instrument’s parts as one component, with an
        embed for study pages.
      </li>
      <li>
        A configuration contract. Today each example hard-codes its ship, start
        and targets. The instrument needs one validated document: ship and
        loadout, start, targets, the opening burn, view and time, and which
        values the designer may change.
      </li>
      <li>
        Room for a study’s own values in the menu, as the Motion instrument
        gives a study its pane.
      </li>
      <li>
        Its own specification and test. The browser test that exists is tied to
        this page and its sections.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Depends on</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The simulation has no doorway for staging. The instrument moves the ship
        after the simulation is built, postpones the encounter, adds wrecks to
        the encounter’s list and gives shards a lifetime, all by writing to
        fields the game happens to leave open. A change inside the game can
        break any of these without warning. That is accepted (10 November 2026):
        the instrument is a tool for studies, it stages from the Lab side, and
        its own test is the guard that catches a break. The game gains no
        staging code for it.
      </li>
      <li>
        An embedded instrument is live only while its study runs. A finished
        study keeps captured images or video of what its instruments showed, so
        later changes to the instrument or the game do not matter to it.
        Replacing live instruments with captures when a study ends is intended
        and not yet practised.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Risks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Engine light is still a ladder of stamps.</strong>
        Seen again on 10 November at Follow 4× after the layer split. Until that
        fault is fixed, Follow 4× shows it in every turn, and a study cannot
        judge an engine trail there.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A jump may not equal play.</strong>
        A jump paints light once per tick; play paints once per drawn frame. The
        two were never compared pixel by pixel, and they can differ whenever a
        frame covers more or less than one tick.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Bloom size against the picture.</strong>
        The bloom is set in screen pixels and the instrument scales its whole
        screen to fit its band. Whether the glow is the same size against the
        ship as in the game at a given window was not checked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cost.</strong>
        The 0.2 s jump was measured before the layer split and the bloom. A page
        with several instruments runs several simulations and filters; none of
        that was measured since.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A burn can end early.</strong>
        From the run’s start B3 flies into the sun at about 10 s. The scene says
        so, but a configuration has to choose a start its burns survive.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Unresolved</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Whether the follow zooms, the held wreck’s distance and its shard
        lifetime are fixed or part of the configuration.
      </li>
      <li>
        The values menu opens over the right half of the scene. Accepted in
        02.4; a study that changes values constantly may want it narrower.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Not checked in this study: the page at 1280 pixels wide, playback speed in
      a visible window, and B2 and B3 in every view. Nothing found blocks
      building the instrument. The stamp fault limits what it can be trusted to
      show at 4× and is fixed elsewhere, not here.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section aria-labelledby="scope">
    <h2 id="scope">04 / One component, its reference page and its embed</h2>
    <p>
      The target is the Flight scene instrument as settled in part 02 and
      referenced by the 02.4 example: one shared component driven by one
      validated configuration, a Lab page that shows it in its default setting,
      and an embed for study pages. It stages scenes from the Lab side; the
      game’s code does not change.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Design-principle disclosure:</strong>
      no change to
      <code>docs/design.md</code>
      is proposed. The instrument is a study tool and changes nothing a player
      sees. Writing this scope does not start the work.
    </p>

    <h3>1 / Configuration and shared component — medium</h3>
    <p>
      Define the configuration as a Zod schema first: loadout, start position
      and heading, placed targets, the held target with its distance and shard
      lifetime, the two follow zooms, and the opening burn, view and time. The
      defaults are the working values of part 02: zooms 1.6 and 4, 140 units,
      two seconds. Move the scene engine out of the study’s components and build
      one instrument component from the 02.4 screen that parses that document,
      with a slot for a study’s own values in the menu. The study’s four
      examples keep running against the moved engine.
    </p>
    <p>
      Owners: a new
      <code>apps/web/src/lib/flight-scene/</code>
      for the schema, starter document and engine, and a component beside the
      Motion instrument’s parts. Write
      <code>docs/specs/lab/flight-scene-instrument/spec.md</code>
      in the same change. Checks, as relations: an invalid document is refused;
      flying to an instant twice gives the same simulation state; a backward
      jump equals a fresh flight to the same instant; the held target stays
      within the cannon’s reach for a whole burn; its shards are gone after
      their lifetime while a placed wreck’s remain. No new dependency.
    </p>

    <h3>2 / Lab reference page — small</h3>
    <p>
      Depends on slice 1. Add a Lab page that mounts the instrument with the
      starter document and nothing else, registered in the Lab’s page map beside
      the Motion instrument’s. It is the place to see the instrument’s default
      look and controls. Move the browser test from this study’s page to it: the
      three layers mount with the game’s blending and bloom, the scene plays and
      pauses, a jump lands on the requested instant, each view draws, each layer
      hides, at 1280 and 1440 pixels wide. Update the Lab specification’s list
      of instruments.
    </p>

    <h3>3 / Study embed — small</h3>
    <p>
      Depends on slice 1. Add the embed a study page uses: the full-window band
      that snaps into place, sharing its rules with the Motion instrument’s
      embed instead of repeating them, and passing the study’s configuration and
      menu content through. Name the instrument in the design-study procedure
      next to the Motion instrument, as the tool for judging how something looks
      in flight. That procedure is a factory document; confirm before changing
      it.
    </p>

    <h3>4 / Close what readiness left unmeasured — small</h3>
    <p>
      Depends on slice 2, which gives a page to measure on. Compare a jump with
      continuous play pixel by pixel at several instants; compare the bloom’s
      size against the ship with the game’s at the same window; measure a jump
      to the end of a burn and a page holding three instruments. Record the
      results in the instrument’s specification. A difference between jump and
      play is a finding to report, not something this slice repairs.
    </p>

    <h3>Order and limits</h3>
    <p>
      Slice 1 first; 2 and 3 are independent of each other; 4 last. Outside this
      scope: the engine-light stamp fault, moving earlier studies onto the
      instrument, replacing a finished study’s live instruments with captures,
      flying by hand, burns with weapon inputs and a comparison of two ships on
      one screen. Until the stamp fault is fixed, the reference page and every
      study using Follow 4× will show it.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

<style is:global>
/* A page holding the instrument snaps to it while scrolling past. */
html:has(.bleed) {
  scroll-snap-type: y proximity;
  overflow-x: clip;
}
</style>
<style>
summary {
  cursor: pointer;
}
section > p,
section > ul {
  max-width: 72ch;
}
/* The prose column's width; the stamp's own type is smaller. */
.stamp {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  max-width: 41rem;
  margin-top: var(--spacing-lg);
  padding: var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-lg);
  border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
}
.stamp table {
  border-collapse: collapse;
}
.stamp th,
.stamp td {
  padding: var(--spacing-sm) var(--spacing-md) var(--spacing-sm) 0;
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
}
.stamp tbody th {
  white-space: nowrap;
  font-weight: normal;
  color: var(--color-text);
}
/* Full window band: the instrument fills it and snaps to its centre. */
.bleed {
  width: 100vw;
  height: 100svh;
  margin-inline: calc(50% - 50vw);
  scroll-snap-align: center;
}
</style>
